EXT. MAYA'S WORKSHOP - DAY

Rain pours over the small town of Whispering Pines. Water rushes down the gutters.

INT. MAYA'S WORKSHOP - DAY

Inside, the air smells of machine oil and dry wood. 

MAYA (9) sits on a high wooden workbench stool. She is a South Asian girl with curly hair tied high. She wears a teal windbreaker and fingerless gloves. Around her neck hangs a small adjustable wrench on a lanyard.

Next to the stool sits her lightweight manual wheelchair. It features bright orange spoke guards and a compact toolkit strapped under the seat.

Maya has a spinal cord injury and is a proud wheelchair user. 

She holds a metal wheel rim. She uses her wrench to tighten a loose spoke. It clicks into place.

MAYA
(to herself)
Perfect tension. Now, if I only had the bearing.

She looks at a blueprint pinned to the wall. It shows a detailed drawing of her wheelchair wheel with a glowing, frictionless ceramic bearing at the center.

MAYA
With the ceramic bearing from the old mill, my push-rims will spin twice as fast. I will fly.

A siren wails in the distance. 

Maya rolls her eyes. She slides off the high workbench stool. She transfers smoothly into her wheelchair seat. Her movements are quick and practiced. 

She rolls to the window and pushes it open. 

EXT. STREET - DAY

The canal at the end of the street has overflowed. A river of clean, rushing water now fills the road. It forms a perfect channel of flumes and ramps.

Other kids run for high ground. 

ROHAN (10) stands on his porch. He holds a sleek, neon-green kayak.

ROHAN
The old mill is flooded! The water is five feet deep!

Maya smiles. Her eyes light up. She looks at her orange wheels. 

MAYA
Five feet? That is a perfect water lane.

She grabs her adjustable wrench totem on her lanyard. She pats it for luck.

INT. MAYA'S WORKSHOP - DAY

Maya rolls back to her workbench. She reaches under her seat and pulls out two curved, orange plastic fins. They are her own invention: Aqua-Flaps.

She transfers onto the floor, sitting comfortably on a soft mat. She clips the Aqua-Flaps onto her wheelchair's large rear wheels. 

She transfers back up into her wheelchair seat. She tests the wheel rotation. The fins spin like paddle-wheels.

MAYA
Ready for launch.

EXT. STREET - CONTINUOUS

Maya rolls down her driveway ramp. The water laps at the bottom of the concrete.

Rohan drags his kayak to the edge of the flooded road. He sees Maya.

ROHAN
Maya! What are you doing? The streets are a river!

MAYA
I am going to the mill, Rohan. I need that ceramic bearing.

ROHAN
In that? You will get stuck! Let me push you to the dry sidewalk.

Rohan reaches out to grab Maya's wheelchair push-rims.

Maya swerves quickly out of his reach. Her eyes are sharp.

MAYA
Hands off, Rohan. My chair is my personal space. I steer.

ROHAN
Fine! But my kayak is a real boat. I will get the bearing first!

Rohan jumps into his kayak. He paddles hard. He shoots down the flooded street.

Maya grins. She grips her push-rims.

MAYA
We will see about that.

She rolls forward. Her wheels hit the water. 

The Aqua-Flaps scoop the water. With each strong push of her arms, Maya glides forward. She is fast. She is stable.

EXT. CANAL CHANNEL - MOMENTS LATER

The current is swift. Maya navigates the water lanes. She plans three moves ahead.

Up ahead, Rohan paddles furiously. His kayak sways in the wind.

Suddenly, a pile of floating wooden crates blocks the channel. They wedge tightly between two buildings.

Rohan rams into the crates. His kayak gets stuck.

ROHAN
Hey! Out of the way!

He tries to paddle backward, but the current pins him.

Maya approaches the block. She looks to the right. 

A concrete pedestrian ramp leads up to a dry store balcony. It bypasses the crates entirely.

MAYA
Time for a detour.

Maya paddles hard. She angles her wheelchair toward the ramp. 

She pops a small wheelie, lifting her front casters onto the dry concrete. She grips her dry push-rims and rolls up the ramp with high-speed precision.

She zips across the store balcony, rolls down the other side, and splashes back into the water channel.

She looks back at Rohan, who is still pushing against the crates.

MAYA
See you at the mill!

EXT. OLD MILL - DAY

The old brick mill stands in the middle of a swirling pool of water. 

The main double doors are blocked by a massive, fallen oak tree. There is no way in through the front.

Maya circles the building. She spots a round concrete drainage pipe near the base. It is three feet wide.

Water drains slowly out of the pipe. 

Rohan finally arrives, paddling hard. He looks at the fallen tree, then at the pipe.

ROHAN
We can't get in! The doors are blocked!

MAYA
Not all of them.

Maya points to the drainage pipe.

ROHAN
That pipe is tiny! My kayak will never fit.

MAYA
My chair will.

Maya aligns her wheelchair with the pipe. She tucks her elbows in. She rolls straight into the dark concrete tube.

INT. OLD MILL - CONTINUOUS

The inside of the mill is vast and echoey. The water here is shallow, only covering the floor by a few inches.

Maya exits the pipe. She is inside the main warehouse.

High above, on a metal platform, sits the old mill generator. A shiny, silver ceramic bearing glints in the dim light.

MAYA
There you are.

Footsteps splash outside. Rohan climbs through the pipe on foot, leaving his kayak behind. He is soaking wet and shivering. 

ROHAN
Okay, so you got inside. But the generator is up there. 

He points to a steep emergency ramp that leads to the high platform.

The metal stairs next to the ramp have collapsed.

The ramp itself is covered in slick mud and black machine oil from the flood.

Rohan runs to the ramp. He tries to run up. 

His sneakers slip on the oily metal. He slides all the way back to the bottom, landing on his bottom with a splash.

ROHAN
Ow! It is too slippery! There is no grip!

Maya rolls up to the base of the ramp. She inspects the surface. 

Running up the center of the ramp are two parallel steel cart-tracks, used long ago to guide heavy wheeled bins.

MAYA
No grip for shoes. But perfect grip for wheels.

She aligns her orange wheelchair wheels with the steel tracks.

ROHAN
Maya, don't! You will slide back down!

MAYA
My wheels are engineered for this.

Maya pushes her wheels forward. Her rubber tires lock perfectly into the steel guide rails of the cart-track.

She grips her push-rims. Because of her spinal cord injury, her upper body is exceptionally strong. She leans forward, keeping her center of gravity low.

She pushes. One stroke. Two strokes.

Her wheels grip the steel tracks. She does not slip.

Rohan watches, his mouth open in awe.

ROHAN
She is actually climbing it.

Maya focuses on her rhythm. Push. Hold. Push. Hold. 

Her arms move with steady, powerful force. Her lightweight chair glides up the steep, oily incline, locked securely in the rails.

She reaches the top platform.

INT. HIGH PLATFORM - CONTINUOUS

Maya rolls onto the dry metal platform. 

She sits right next to the old generator. She pulls her small adjustable wrench from her lanyard.

With three quick turns, she unscrews the housing. She reaches inside and pulls out the frictionless ceramic bearing. It is pristine, spinning silently in her hand.

MAYA
Got you.

A loud crack echoes through the mill. 

Below, the support beams groan under the weight of the floodwaters.

ROHAN
Maya! The wall is shifting! We have to go!

Maya looks down. The drainage pipe is starting to crumble.

She looks at the steep ramp. 

MAYA
Hold on tight, Rohan! Get to the exit!

Maya tucks her wrench back onto her lanyard. She grips her push-rims and lines her wheels up with the steel cart-tracks.

She releases her brakes.

Maya flies down the ramp. It is a thrilling, high-speed ride. She balances perfectly, steering with micro-adjustments of her hands.

She shoots off the bottom of the ramp, flying over the shallow water and landing with a massive splash right near the drainage pipe.

She grabs Rohan's hand as she passes.

MAYA
Move!

They scramble through the concrete pipe just as a heavy timber falls behind them, sealing the entrance.

EXT. OLD MILL - DAY

Maya and Rohan splash out into the open water. The rain has stopped. The floodwaters are already beginning to recede.

Rohan sits in his kayak, catching his breath. He looks at Maya. He smiles, shaking his head.

ROHAN
I thought I was the fast one. But you... you are on another level.

MAYA
It is all in the wheels, Rohan. And the planning.

She holds up the shiny ceramic bearing. It sparkles in the breaking sunlight.

INT. MAYA'S WORKSHOP - EVENING

The water outside has returned to the canal. The streets are dry.

Maya sits in her manual wheelchair. She is still a proud wheelchair user, her physical trait unchanged.

She finishes tightening the axle of her right wheel. 

At the center of her wheel, the new ceramic bearing spins. It is silent. It spins and spins without stopping.

Maya grins. She pushes her rims once. 

Her wheelchair glides across the workshop floor with effortless, incredible speed. She stops on a dime, turning to face the window.

MAYA
Next race, I am ready.

She pats her adjustable wrench totem on her chest. 

FADE OUT.